            <title xml:id="title_282N2017C" xml:lang="da">Geistlig Ouverture for Orchester og Orgel ad libitum</title>
            <title xml:id="title_fb912d28" xml:lang="en">Sacred Overture for Orchestra and Organ ad libitum</title>
            <title type="alternative" xml:id="title_d13cec79" xml:lang="da">Concert Ouverture</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_N20388">
               <persName role="composer" xml:id="persName_282N20187">J.P.E. Hartmann</persName>
            </contributor>
            <creation xml:id="creation_282N2018C">
               <date isodate="1827-04-04" xml:id="date_N20391">1827-04-04</date>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_282N2018A">
               <eventList type="performances" xml:id="eventList_2abc08b5">
                  <event xml:id="event_d1e644632">
                     <date isodate="1839-05-25" xml:id="date_N20442">1839-05-25</date>
                     <geogName role="venue" xml:id="geogName_282N20240">Frederiks Tyske Kirke (Christianskirken)</geogName>
                     <geogName role="place" xml:id="geogName_282N20244">Copenhagen</geogName>
                     <persName role="conductor" xml:id="persName_282N2024B">Ivar Bredal</persName>
                     <desc xml:id="idm101">Concert given by De Massmannske Søndagsskoler.</desc>
                  </event>
               </eventList>
               <p xml:id="idm102">In 1881 Hartmann told Angul Hammerich that the overture had had its first performance at Trinitatis Kirke in Copenhagen conducted by Giuseppe Siboni in connection with De Massmannske Søndagsskolers Stiftelsesfest. However, the only known performance took place in Frederiks Tyske Kirke on 25 May 1839 two months after the death of Siboni. This concert was conducted by Ivar Bredal who, according to an advertisement in <rend xml:id="idm103" fontstyle="italic">Kiøbenhavns Kongelig alene priviligerede Adresse-Contoirs Efterretninger, </rend>held a rehearsal on that day in Trinitatis Kirke. For performances in Det Harmoniske Selskab and Euterpe, both housed at Knirsch (the present Hotel d'Angleterre) where there was no organ available, Hartmann incorporated the organ part in the other instruments, primarily the woodwinds.</p>
            </history>
